  "prediction": "**Pacific Crest Trail Sees Increased Interest and Crowding**\n\nThe Pacific Crest Trail (PCT) has seen a significant increase in interest and crowding in recent years, thanks in part to the success of Cheryl Strayed's memoir \"Wild\" and the subsequent movie adaptation. The PCTA, which preserves and promotes the trail, has reported a 300% increase in website traffic and a surge in requests for permits for long-distance hikes.\n\nAccording to the PCTA, more than 4,000 long-distance hiking permits have been issued this year, with over 3,000 of those permits being for northbound thru-hikers. This is a significant increase from previous years, with only 300 people attempting to hike the entire trail in 2006. The current permit system, which was introduced in 2015, aims to spread the number of hikers out and mitigate wear on the trail and environmental damage.\n\nHowever, the increased crowding has raised concerns about the impact on the trail and its fragile ecosystems. The US Forest Service has reported 20 rescues in the first 50 miles of the trail, and there are concerns about degraded vegetation, establishment of new camp spots, and human waste. The PCTA has launched a campaign, #responsiblywild, to promote safety and \"leave no trace\" practices.\n\nDespite the challenges, many hikers are drawn to the PCT for its transformative power. The trail has been shown to have a profound impact on hikers, helping them to heal and find themselves. However, some hikers are criticized for their lack of preparation and experience, which can put themselves and others at risk.\n\nThe PCTA is working to address the issue of overcrowding and to promote responsible hiking practices. The organization is encouraging hikers to plan ahead, be prepared, and respect the trail and its environment. With careful planning and responsible behavior, the PCT can continue to be a transformative and life-changing experience for hikers.\n\n**Key Statistics:**\n\n* 300% increase in website traffic\n* 4,000 long-distance hiking permits issued this year\n* 3,000 northbound thru-hikers\n* 20 rescues in the first 50 miles of the trail\n* 50 permits per day cap\n* 2,800 long-distance permits issued in 2015\n* 1,200 hikers made it to Canada in 2015\n\n**Recommendations:**\n\n* Plan ahead and be prepared\n* Respect the trail and its environment\n* Follow \"",
